WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.755
Why is noon sunshine so much stronger than evening sunshine, when it's the very same sun?

00:00:06.105 --> 00:00:11.779
And why does a skylight three times wider make a room almost nine times brighter, not three?

00:00:12.129 --> 00:00:17.185
Both answers come from one idea, and it sits at the heart of the rendering equation.

00:00:17.535 --> 00:00:25.194
Here's our test room. A courtyard with a round patch of bright sky overhead, and a white card on the floor measuring the light.

00:00:26.394 --> 00:00:29.274
First, how big is a light, as seen from the card?

00:00:29.624 --> 00:00:39.373
Not its size in metres, and not how many pixels it covers. What matters is how much of the sky it fills. That's its solid angle, measured in steradians.

00:00:39.723 --> 00:00:47.510
A whole sphere of directions is four pi steradians, about twelve and a half. Half a sphere is two pi, a bit over six.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:55.817
Our patch of sky is a round cap. Its solid angle is two pi, times one minus the cosine of its angular radius.

00:00:57.017 --> 00:01:05.871
Now the skylight test. Same sky brightness, same camera. Only the cap's angular radius changes, from five degrees to fifteen.

00:01:06.221 --> 00:01:09.634
The cap ends up covering close to nine times as much sky.

00:01:09.984 --> 00:01:13.632
And the light on the card goes up by almost exactly that much.

00:01:13.982 --> 00:01:21.918
That's the skylight puzzle. Three times wider in angle means about nine times more sky, so about nine times more light.

00:01:23.118 --> 00:01:27.619
But the light grows a touch less than the sky does. The reason is a cosine.

00:01:27.969 --> 00:01:35.799
Light arriving at a slant spreads over more floor, so each direction counts in proportion to the cosine of its angle from straight up.

00:01:36.149 --> 00:01:41.375
Weight every bit of solid angle by that cosine, and you get the projected solid angle.

00:01:41.725 --> 00:01:48.701
For the cap, it's pi, times the sine of the radius, squared. Think of it as the cap's shadow, flattened onto the floor.

00:01:49.051 --> 00:01:58.822
The light on the card doesn't follow the solid angle. It follows the projected solid angle. That cosine is the whole story of noon versus evening sun.

00:02:00.022 --> 00:02:06.977
We tested that directly. Here's the card's light against projected solid angle, across lots of cap sizes.

00:02:07.327 --> 00:02:13.919
The measured points sit exactly on a straight line. Light equals sky brightness times projected solid angle.

00:02:14.269 --> 00:02:20.818
The red ghost line uses plain solid angle, forgetting the cosine, and it drifts away as the cap grows.

00:02:21.168 --> 00:02:30.747
For a small cap the two measures agree almost perfectly. For the biggest one they're a couple of percent apart. The bigger the light, the more the cosine matters.

00:02:31.947 --> 00:02:36.576
Now tilt the card instead. Keep the same sky, and lean the card well over.

00:02:36.926 --> 00:02:41.363
The sky didn't get darker. The card just sees it at a slant, and catches less.

00:02:41.713 --> 00:02:46.151
The cosine is geometry. It's not a brightness slider you get to tune.

00:02:46.501 --> 00:02:52.005
It's exactly why winter sun feels weak, and why solar panels are angled toward the sun.

00:02:53.205 --> 00:02:57.279
One more consequence. A whole hemisphere is two pi steradians.

00:02:57.629 --> 00:03:01.725
But weight it by the cosine, and the projected hemisphere is just pi.

00:03:02.075 --> 00:03:12.742
So a uniform sky of brightness L puts L times pi on a flat card, not L times two pi. That pi, in every Lambert formula, comes from right here.

00:03:13.942 --> 00:03:18.251
Finally, a path tracer estimates this integral with random directions.

00:03:18.601 --> 00:03:28.927
Sampling evenly over solid angle, or in proportion to the cosine, both get there, as long as each sample is divided by the probability it was drawn with.

00:03:29.277 --> 00:03:34.610
As the sample count climbs toward a thousand, the noise melts away and the error falls.

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:36.941
A few limits.

00:03:37.291 --> 00:03:41.835
One courtyard, one round sky cap, one matte card, and a software renderer.

00:03:42.185 --> 00:03:49.097
Every number comes from the exact formulas and the estimator, not from the pictures. Every check in the run passed.

00:03:50.297 --> 00:03:52.067
To sum it up in three lines.

00:03:52.417 --> 00:03:56.727
The size of a light means solid angle, not metres, and not pixels.

00:03:57.077 --> 00:04:02.474
Surfaces respond to projected solid angle, the solid angle weighted by the cosine.

00:04:02.824 --> 00:04:10.568
That's the cosine inside the rendering equation, written down by James Kajiya in nineteen eighty-six. Thanks for watching.

